<edge source="russia_ukraine_conflict::conflict_intensity" target="mid_east_energy::shipping_bottleneck_inde" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A sharp rise in kinetic operations around the Black Sea and adjacent maritime approaches increases attacks/insurance costs and forces tankers to reroute via Suez/Red Sea or reduce sailings, producing congestion and port/backlog pressures in Middle Eastern transit corridors.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">mid_east_energy</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_conflict::conflict_intensity" target="space_comm_governance::political_intervention" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Sharp upticks in kinetic operations prompt governments to impose export controls, sanctions, and direct orders (e.g., service denials or routing restrictions) on satellite and space-communications firms to deny support to battlefield actors, increasing explicit political interventions on provider behavior.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">space_comm_governance</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_conflict::conflict_intensity" target="space_comm_governance::military_service_reliab" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased conflict intensity produces more kinetic, electronic-warfare and anti-satellite activity as well as deliberate jamming and cyberattacks against ground stations and links, directly degrading uptime, latency and throughput of comms used for military missions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">space_comm_governance</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_conflict::conflict_intensity" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::regulatory_stringency" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in kinetic operations and visible battlefield use of dual‑use technologies triggers immediate national security reviews and rapid expansion of export controls and oversight regimes that tighten AI‑related regulation affecting US–China tech interactions.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_conflict::conflict_intensity" target="western_bond_volatility::safe_haven_asset_demand" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">A spike in kinetic operations raises global risk aversion and prompts institutional and retail investors to reallocate into high-quality liquid assets (e.g., US Treasuries, German bunds), increasing safe-haven demand.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">western_bond_volatility</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_conflict::conflict_intensity" target="western_bond_volatility::sovereign_spread" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising battlefield intensity heightens perceived fiscal and economic exposure for regionally linked Western sovereigns (energy disruption, refugee costs, trade frictions), so investors demand higher spreads over benchmark sovereigns.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">western_bond_volatility</data>
</edge>
